<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="sec_conceptual_check_dataset_bias" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Conceptual Check: Dataset Bias</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>A team trains a behavioral cloning model for a robot using demonstration data collected entirely from a single practice course that only ever required right turns.</p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex_conceptual_check_dataset_bias">
    <statement>
      <p>What will most likely happen when this robot is deployed on a new course that requires left turns?</p>
    </statement>
    <choices>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>The model will perform equally well on left and right turns, since steering is symmetric.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Behavioral cloning models learn from the specific examples they were shown; steering symmetry in the physical world does not transfer to a model that never saw a left-turn example.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>The model will be biased toward turning right and will struggle to execute left turns.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Correct. Because the model only ever saw examples where the correct action was to turn right, it has no demonstrated examples of left-turn behavior to imitate. Behavioral cloning models reproduce patterns present in their training data; they do not automatically generalize to maneuvers that were absent from that data.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>The model will refuse to move at all on any new course.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>The model will still produce steering outputs on new input; it just will not have learned the correct behavior for turns unlike anything in its training set.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>The model's performance will depend only on image resolution, not on the training data.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Image resolution can affect performance, but the dominant failure mode here comes from what maneuvers were and were not represented in the training demonstrations.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>
  <p>Fixing this requires collecting balanced demonstrations that include left turns, not just improving model architecture.</p>
</section>
